<h2>From a kidnapping FIR to POCSO charges</h2>
<p>The FIR was registered in July 2024 at PS Bhalswa Dairy — lodged, remarkably, by the first petitioner herself, the mother of the minor victim, alleging kidnapping. The investigation inverted the narrative. In her statement under Section 183 of the BNSS, the victim alleged that between December 2023 and July 2024 her mother had coerced her into sexual relations with the second petitioner and was present during the assaults, and that a recording existed on the mother’s phone. The chargesheet followed in November 2024, and in January 2025 the trial court framed charges under Sections 70(2), 351(3) and 64(2)(m) of the BNS and Section 6 of the POCSO Act against both.</p>

<h2>The question of law</h2>
<p>The petitioners’ challenge rested on a proposition with respectable ancestry: rape under Section 63 BNS — like Section 375 IPC before it — can be committed only by a man; therefore, they argued, the “group of persons” contemplated by the gang rape provision can only be a group of men, and a woman cannot be charged. They invoked the Supreme Court’s decisions in <em>Priya Patel</em> and <em>Hemraj</em>.</p>
<p>The amicus curiae appointed by the Court offered the counter-architecture. After the Criminal Law (Amendment) Act, 2013, gang rape ceased to be an explanation appended to the rape provision and became a standalone offence — Section 376D IPC, now Section 70 BNS: where a woman is raped by one or more persons constituting a group or acting in furtherance of a common intention, each of those persons shall be deemed to have committed the offence of rape. <em>Priya Patel</em> and <em>Hemraj</em>, decided under the pre-2013 text, do not control the amended scheme.</p>

<h2>What the Court held — and what it did not</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Held</strong><p>There is no basis to hold that a woman cannot be charged with gang rape under Section 70 BNS or Section 376D IPC. The deeming provision creates a standalone offence reaching all who form the group or act in furtherance of the common intention, regardless of gender.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Limited</strong><p>A woman alone cannot be charged with gang rape: the group’s act must still involve rape as defined in Section 63 BNS, which — as currently construed — requires a male principal.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Left open</strong><p>Whether the word “man” in Section 63 could ever be read gender-neutrally was expressly not decided, and the Court noted the broader question may be revisited in an appropriate case.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>POCSO</strong><p>The Section 6 POCSO charge against the mother was sustained on the strength of Sundari Gautam, which reads the offence of penetrative sexual assault as gender-neutral.</p></div>
</div>
<p>On that footing the charge order of 17 January 2025 was upheld and the revision dismissed.</p>

<h2>Why the decision matters</h2>
<p>Prosecutions arising from intra-family sexual abuse frequently allege facilitation by an adult woman of the household — procuring, coercing, standing guard. Before this ruling, defence challenges in Delhi could lean on <em>Priya Patel</em> to argue that no gang rape charge could touch a woman at all, leaving only abetment. The judgment closes that route for the post-2013 framework: participation in the group act, with common intention, is itself enough for the principal charge. Combined with the gender-neutral reach of the POCSO Act — whose Section 6 carries a minimum of twenty years’ rigorous imprisonment — the charge-stage exposure of facilitators has materially changed.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>The ruling operates at the stage of charge: it decides what the accused must answer at trial, not guilt. The allegations remain to be proved by evidence, and the identity of the minor victim remains protected under Section 23 of the POCSO Act and Section 72 of the BNS.</p>
</div>
